Topi Linkala wrote:
> 
> I didn't say that there is freedom of speech anywhere. What I said that 
> if we agree on freedom of speech then it is an absolute. You either have 
> it or you don't.

Well.. - not quite. In abstract theory, maybe, where "tertium non datur"
is asserted.

Many legislations have concepts more like Kant's Categorical Imperative.

Your "unbounded freedom" to act as you like ends where you have contact
with other "entities" of the society and where you hurt them in their
(maybe constitutional) rights (like their "human dignity" in the German
constitution).

To illustrate (correct me and blame me for all inaccuracies in translation);

The German constitution says in article 5 (freedom of speed, etc.)
and mind paragraph 2 where offenses/insults are covered to protect
the residents...
  (1) Everyone has the right to tell his opinions by speech, written and
  through pictures [...]. There's no censorship.
  (2) These rights have their limits in [...] legislative regulations for
  legal protection of children and _in the right of personal honor_.
  (3) [...]

Quite similar article 2 (freedom to act)...
and mind the part where your rights don't take precedence over other
residents...
  (1) Everyone has the right of free development of his individuality,
  _as long as he does not violate rights of others_, acts against the
  consitutional regime or acts against good comity.
  (2) [...]

There are limits in absolute concepts of such things like "freedom to act
as you like" and also in freedom of speech at the point where you meet and
coexist with other individuals.

> Btw. why would offensive speech not to be allowed? I've offended chucar 
> several times in this newsgroup. I've even called him a liar. But 
> wouldn't your freedom of speech allow this?

First of all, I think it's good practise to avoid such attributions like
"liar"; in my ear that sounds like asserting that he says the untruth
intentionally (which might be the case -maybe even pathological- or not,
it doesn't matter).

For practical purpose the internet is ideal to hide behind it's anonymity
and international decentralized (thus legally incoherent) architecture.
Sueing is hardly done for such trivialities, as far as I can observe.
For good "style points" (at least) one should abstain from insults or
offenses.

